WEBVTT

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:03.560
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，欢迎收听《美国生活》。

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>How many shows do you think you've started by saying, "So tell me what happened"?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你觉得你开始节目时用“所以，告诉我发生了什么”这句话，开了多少期节目？

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:08.600
<v Ira Glass>It's actually very few.
<v Ira Glass>其实没多少。

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:16.570
<v Ira Glass>That's Chana Joffe-Walt, one of our producers. But this week, when she came into the studio with me, she did call this one correctly. What else could I say?
<v Ira Glass>那是我们的制片人之一，查娜·乔夫-沃尔特。但本周，当她和我一起走进录音室时，她确实说对了这一点。我还能说什么呢？

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.160
<v Ira Glass>So what did happen?
<v Ira Glass>所以到底发生了什么？

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:33.770
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>OK, so this was a few weeks ago. Elementary and middle schools in East Harlem all got together in an auditorium for a talent show, a school talent show. So little kids are coming up to the stage to do orchestra performances and band and dance performances.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>好吧，那是几周前的事。东哈莱姆区的小学和初中生们聚集在一个礼堂里参加才艺表演，学校的才艺表演。所以小孩子们上台进行管弦乐表演、乐队表演和舞蹈表演。

00:00:33.770 --> 00:01:01.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And at the end of the night, there's one more act on the program. It says "special surprise performance." And a drag queen comes out on stage, a grown-up. She's wearing this long, red wig and a black sequined leotard that has sheer arms with feathers around her wrists. And she comes out and does an Iris Chacón number, this Puerto Rican icon.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在表演结束时，节目单上还有一个节目。上面写着“特别惊喜表演”。然后一位变装皇后走上舞台，是个成年人。她戴着一顶长长的红假发，穿着一件黑色亮片连体衣，袖子是透明的，手腕处有羽毛。她走出来，表演了波多黎各偶像艾丽丝·查孔的曲目。

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.730
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[CHEERING]
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[欢呼声]

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.890
<v Ira Glass>And this is the sound of that?
<v Ira Glass>这就是那个声音吗？

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:23.360
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So this is the sound of that. This is actually the sound of when she gets down on the ground and rolls over onto all fours then rolls back onto her back and puts her two legs up in the air and then opens them. And you can see her underwear.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这就是那个声音。这其实是她趴在地上，翻身成四肢着地，然后又翻回背上，双腿向上并在空中张开时的声音。你可以看到她的内裤。

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.660
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[WILD LAUGHTER]
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[疯狂的笑声]

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.310
<v Ira Glass>So just to be clear, there are elementary school kids here?
<v Ira Glass>为了明确起见，那里有小学生在场吗？

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:56.660
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yes! Yes. This is a school performance. There are tons of kids. And on the program, it doesn't say who this person is, but it turns out a lot of people in this school know this person because he is a well-known dad in the school. He's the president of the PTA. Longtime president. This is actually his very last term as PTA president. He's the outgoing PTA president.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有！有的。这是一场学校演出。有很多孩子。节目单上没写这个人是谁，但事实证明，学校里的很多人都认识这个人，因为他是学校里一位有名的父亲。他是家长教师协会（PTA）的主席。长期担任主席。这实际上是他作为PTA主席的最后一届任期了。他是即将卸任的PTA主席。

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:04.790
<v Ira Glass>Chana tried to reach the guy, but never talked to him. She actually heard about this story in a tabloid newspaper, The Daily News, under this headline--
<v Ira Glass>查娜试图联系这个人，但从未和他谈过。她实际上是在一份小报《每日新闻》上听说的这个故事，标题是——

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:25.390
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>"Parents Horrified After Man Performs Surprise Drag Show at Manhattan School Talent Event." And that article has descriptions like, "Parents said families' laughter turned to disbelief and then dismay as Quinones"-- the PTA dad-- "opened his mouth and exercised his tongue in a suggestive manner while lip-syncing the Chacn number."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>“男子在曼哈顿学校才艺活动中表演突袭式变装秀，家长们感到震惊。”那篇文章有这样的描述：“家长们说，当基尼奥内斯——那位PTA爸爸——在对口型演唱查孔的歌曲时，张开嘴并以暗示性的方式活动舌头，家庭们的笑声变成了难以置信，随之而来的是沮丧。”

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:29.210
<v Reporter>School talent show in Manhattan taking a raunchy turn--
<v Reporter>曼哈顿的学校才艺表演变得低俗——

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:35.840
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So the story spreads to TV. It has a brief, enthusiastic moment on local TV.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以这个故事传到了电视上。它在当地电视台上获得了一个简短而热烈的瞬间。

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.700
<v Reporter>This outlandish drag performance, put on by the PTA president--
<v Reporter>这场由PTA主席上演的奇异变装表演——

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And all the TV reporters talked to this same mother, the one who complained about it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所有的电视记者都采访了同一位母亲，就是那位投诉此事的母亲。

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:55.580
<v Raquel Morales>He laid on his back. He raised his legs. Opened his legs wide open. What made you, in your head, think that that was appropriate for elementary school students?
<v Raquel Morales>他躺在背上。他抬起腿。把双腿张得很大。你脑子里怎么会认为这适合小学生？

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:02.060
<v Reporter>Mrs. Morales' report card for both the school and the district? An F and a triple X.
<v Reporter>莫拉莱斯夫人给学校和学区打出的成绩单是什么？一个F和一个三级X。

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.360
<v Ira Glass>That is perfect local TV newswriting, I have to say.
<v Ira Glass>不得不说，那是完美的当地电视新闻写作。

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.430
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It was like she was waiting for the chance.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>就好像她一直在等待机会一样。

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:08.960
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:16.280
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It was clear from some of the other coverage that lots of people in the school like this guy, know him really well, and that some people liked the performance.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>从其他一些报道中可以看出，学校里很多人都喜欢这个家伙，对他很了解，而且有些人喜欢这场表演。

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:20.930
<v Audience Member>The performance was spot-on. Spot on. He did everything like Iris Chacn would have done.
<v Audience Member>表演非常到位。非常到位。他做得就像艾丽丝·查孔会做的那样。

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.010
<v Vanessa Murdock>Was it OK in this environment?
<v Vanessa Murdock>在这种环境下，这样做合适吗？

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.950
<v Audience Member>I think they've seen a lot worse.
<v Audience Member>我想他们见过更糟糕的。

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:32.040
<v Vanessa Murdock>Have they? From East Harlem, Vanessa Murdock, CBS2 News.
<v Vanessa Murdock>是吗？来自东哈莱姆区，凡妮莎·默多克，CBS2新闻报道。

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:38.870
<v Ira Glass>OK. So you heard about this thing that happened, and it interested you. It hit something in you, and your reaction was different.
<v Ira Glass>好的。你听说了这件事，这引起了你的兴趣。它触动了你内心的某些东西，你的反应与众不同。

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:59.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It totally captivated me. I thought about him so much. And I did not have any of those reactions. It didn't feel like it was super scandalous. I just felt totally taken by it because I spend a lot of time watching shows like this. I have two young kids. I have younger sisters. I do reporting in schools.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它完全吸引了我。我非常关注他。我没有任何那种反应。我觉得这并不算特别丑闻。我只是被它完全吸引了，因为我花了很多时间看这样的节目。我有两个年幼的孩子。我有年幼的姐妹。我在学校里做报道。

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:10.760
<v Ira Glass>In fact, in that same month as the drag performance, Chana went to a preschool end of the year ceremony and performance, a kindergarten stepping up ceremony, a middle school graduation, a middle school talent show, and a magic after-school program performance.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，就在变装表演的那个月，查娜参加了一个学前班的年终庆典和表演、幼儿园结业典礼、初中毕业典礼、初中才艺表演以及课后魔术项目表演。

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:31.180
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That was just June. And that was when I saw this video. And I feel like I spend most of the time sitting through those shows just waiting for something like this to happen, like maybe something exciting and unexpected will happen.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那只是六月。而那是我看到这段视频的时候。我觉得我大部分时间都在这些节目中坐着，只是等待着像这样的事情发生，也许会发生一些令人兴奋和意想不到的事情。

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.210
<v Ira Glass>Because those shows are pretty boring.
<v Ira Glass>因为那些节目真的很无聊。

00:04:33.210 --> 00:05:10.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They're mind-numbing. And then there's the one tiny, tiny moment where your kid does something and no matter what they do, that's amazing, but then you have to continue to perform to be as amazed at every other kid for the next, sometimes, three hours. So all I ever hope for is something in one of these shows will happen that is unexpected. And I know this is not the surprise that people in this school wanted. Like, I get that. It is sexual. There are children. It's drag. It's gay, which is probably even scarier to some parents. That whole thing, I understand. I get that.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它们让人麻木。然后有一个极其微小的瞬间，你的孩子做了一些事情，无论他们做什么，那都是了不起的，但随后你必须在接下来的、有时长达三个小时的时间里，继续表现出对其他每一个孩子同样惊叹的样子。所以我唯一希望的就是在这些节目中能发生一些意想不到的事情。我知道这不是这所学校里的人想要的惊喜。比如，我明白这一点。它是色情的。有孩子在场。这是变装。这是同性恋，这对一些家长来说可能更可怕。整个这件事，我理解。我明白。

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:11.040
<v Ira Glass>But.
<v Ira Glass>但是。

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:26.740
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But I feel like the thing I liked about it is not just that it's surprising, but it's him. Like, it is this guy. This is a real thing that is happening. He is getting on stage and sharing a part of himself with his community.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但我感觉我喜欢它的原因不仅仅是因为它令人惊讶，而是因为那是他。就像，这是真实发生的。他走上舞台，与社区分享他自己的一部分。

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:27.730
<v Ira Glass>He's being real.
<v Ira Glass>他表现得很真实。

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:44.590
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He's being real. Yeah, he's being real. And that's what you want, right? He's kind of modeling the thing that you want from these shows, is that some kid will get up there and do some weird, strange thing that is just themselves, that is just true, just feels like who they are.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他表现得很真实。是的，他表现得很真实。这就是你想要的，对吧？他有点像在模拟你从这些节目中想要的东西，那就是某个孩子会走上台去做一些奇怪的、陌生但只是他们自己的事情，那是真实的，感觉就像他们本来的样子。

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:46.960
<v Ira Glass>You're saying that because you've actually seen that?
<v Ira Glass>你这么说是因为你真的见过吗？

00:05:46.960 --> 00:06:21.370
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Well, I was thinking, after watching this video, that one of the most moving things I've ever seen on stage, actually, was at a school talent show years ago that I hadn't thought about for a long time, where this girl got up. She was maybe 10, and she was super shy. She was like one of those kids that you're kind of surprised that she's getting on stage in the first place. And she seemed nervous. And then the music comes on and she very confidently moves into this strange interpretive dance of Billie Jean, where she's--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嗯，我在想，在看了这段视频后，我曾经在舞台上见过的最感人的事情之一，其实是几年前在一次学校才艺表演上，我很久没想过这件事了，当时那个女孩上台了。她可能10岁，非常害羞。她就像那种让你很惊讶她居然会站上舞台的孩子。她看起来很紧张。然后音乐响起，她非常自信地进入了这段奇怪的《Billie Jean》现代舞，在那里她——

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:22.160
<v Ira Glass>Michael Jackson.
<v Ira Glass>迈克尔·杰克逊。

00:06:22.160 --> 00:07:06.470
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah, Michael Jackson, but she's not wearing a fedora. She's wearing a beret. The most striking part of it was just that she was super confident. And she's kind of leaning over like she's like an elderly person and moving her arms back and forth, like octopus arms. I remember she had braids, purple braids, that were also swinging back and forth. And people lost it. Everybody went totally crazy for her. Not because she was good. She wasn't doing the moves very well. But it was her. This was a true thing that was happening. This was a person who the stage gave her a place to perform something that she's interested in, something who she really is, something that just is real, is true.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是的，迈克尔·杰克逊，但她没戴软呢帽。她戴着一顶贝雷帽。最引人注目的是她超级自信。她有点像个老人一样弯着腰，双臂来回摆动，就像章鱼的手臂一样。我记得她扎着辫子，紫色的辫子，也跟着来回摆动。人们疯了。每个人都为她彻底疯狂。不是因为她跳得好。她的动作做得不怎么样。但那是她。这是一件正在发生的真实的事情。这是一个舞台给了她一个表演空间的人，她对某件事感兴趣，那是她真实的样子，是一些真实、纯粹的东西。

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:36.600
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio program, that is what we aspire to. That is where we're heading. We are leaving behind the obligatory, the half-baked, the phoned-in, the superficial. We leave behind the meh. And we turn our backs on the word "meh" because the word "meh" is meh itself. And instead, we have stories of people seeking out what is real, what is true in some places you might not expect that. What you find, what you feel, what you know to be real. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的广播节目中，这就是我们要追求的。这就是我们要前进的方向。我们将抛弃那些义务性的、半生不熟的、敷衍了事的、肤浅的东西。我们抛弃“平庸”。我们拒绝“平庸”这个词，因为“平庸”这个词本身就很平庸。相反，我们讲述人们寻找真实、寻找在某些意想不到的地方所呈现的真相的故事。你所发现的、你所感受的、你所知道的真实。请继续收听。

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:40.900
<v Ira Glass>Act One, How I Learned to Start Worrying and Fear the Bomb.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，我是如何学会停止担忧并害怕炸弹的。

00:07:40.900 --> 00:08:00.150
<v Ira Glass>You probably heard a few weeks ago North Korea fired an intercontinental ballistic missile, one powerful enough, news reports said, to reach Alaska with a nuclear warhead. And it was big news. People were shocked, North Korea being on the verge of being able to nuke us. Which was strange to one of our producers here at our show, David Kestenbaum. He used to be a reporter for National Public Radio.
<v Ira Glass>你可能几周前听说朝鲜发射了一枚洲际弹道导弹，新闻报道称其威力足以携带核弹头到达阿拉斯加。这是个大新闻。人们感到震惊，朝鲜处于能够向我们投掷核弹的边缘。这对我们节目的一位制片人大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆来说很奇怪。他曾经是国家公共电台的记者。

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:14.810
<v David Kestenbaum>When I was at NPR, one of the things I helped cover was nuclear weapons policy around the world. And when it came to North Korea, I've talked to people, various experts, and they would say something like, we have a decade. It's going to take them a while to get there.
<v David Kestenbaum>我在NPR时，我负责报道的内容之一是世界各地的核武器政策。当谈到朝鲜时，我和各种专家谈过，他们会说类似“我们还有十年时间”的话。他们要达到那个目标还需要一段时间。

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.430
<v Ira Glass>And then how long ago was that?
<v Ira Glass>那是多久以前的事了？

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.240
<v David Kestenbaum>About a decade ago.
<v David Kestenbaum>大约十年前。

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.500
<v Ira Glass>So right on time.
<v Ira Glass>所以正好准时。

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:23.950
<v David Kestenbaum>We're exactly on schedule.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们完全按计划进行。

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.360
<v Ira Glass>Right. We said that 10 years ago it would take 10 years. Here we are.
<v Ira Glass>对。我们十年前说过这需要十年。现在我们到了。

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:27.950
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:29.200
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:34.610
<v David Kestenbaum>And I just thought, how did we let this happen? How did we get here?
<v David Kestenbaum>我只是在想，我们怎么会让这种情况发生？我们是怎么走到这一步的？

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:46.660
<v Ira Glass>How did we get here? All of us, David included, were not being real with ourselves about what was going on with North Korea. To remedy that, and to get some answers about how we let this happen, David turned to a trusted source on iTunes.
<v Ira Glass>我们是怎么走到这一步的？我们所有人，包括大卫，在朝鲜问题上都没有对自己诚实。为了纠正这一点，并就我们如何让这种情况发生得到一些答案，大卫转向了iTunes上的一个可信来源。

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:59.950
<v Ira Glass>Quick warning. There are a couple curse words in his story and in one other story in today's program that we have unbeeped here on the internet version of our program. If you don't want to hear those words, maybe you're listening with kids, there is a beeped version at our website. Here's David.
<v Ira Glass>简短警告。他的故事和今天节目中的另一个故事中有几个脏话，我们在我们节目的互联网版本中没有将其消音。如果你不想听到那些话，也许你是和孩子们一起听的，我们的网站上有消音版本。这是大卫的报道。

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:22.150
<v David Kestenbaum>Back when I was covering this stuff, we reporters all had a short list of people we would call to comment when there was news. And often, this thing would happen. When the interview started and we were recording, the person would get super serious because, you know, nuclear weapons. The world could end. The thing is, that is not how a lot of them actually talked about this stuff. The way they actually talked off mic was more like this.
<v David Kestenbaum>当我还在报道这些事情的时候，我们记者都有一个简短的名单，在有新闻时我们会打电话给名单上的人评论。通常情况下，会发生这种情况。采访开始录音时，对方会变得超级严肃，因为你知道，核武器。世界可能会毁灭。问题是，这并不是他们谈论这些事情的真实方式。他们在麦克风外谈论的方式更像是这样。

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:25.420
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Kim Jong Un posed with a nuclear weapon!
<v Jeffrey Lewis>金正恩拿着核武器拍照！

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:26.890
<v Aaron Stein>We saw everything.
<v Aaron Stein>我们看到了所有东西。

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:46.120
<v David Kestenbaum>This is from a podcast called Arms Control Wonk. It's helped me understand North Korea in a way that I never had. It was started by Jeffrey Lewis. He's a nuclear policy expert and also director of the East Asia Nonproliferation Program at the Middlebury Institute of International Studies at Monterey. He says, frankly, he was tired of all the formal diplomat speak.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是来自一个叫《军备控制专家》的播客。它以我从未有过的方式帮助我了解朝鲜。它是由杰弗里·刘易斯发起的。他是一位核政策专家，也是蒙特雷明德国际研究所东亚防扩散项目主任。他说，坦率地说，他厌倦了所有正式的外交辞令。

00:09:46.120 --> 00:10:12.390
<v Jeffrey Lewis>I was giving a talk to the board of Middlebury College. This was a pretty big talk to have to give to show off the research of the center. And at some point, we were talking about a photograph of Kim Jong Un that had been digitally altered. Something had been done to his ears. And we see this a lot. And what I said was, they always fuck with his ears. And everybody was looking at me like, uh, did you just say that?
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我当时在给明德学院的董事会做演讲。这是一个相当重要的演讲，用来展示该中心的研究成果。在某个时候，我们正在谈论一张金正恩被数字化修改过的照片。他的耳朵被做了手脚。我们经常看到这种情况。我说的是，他们总是乱搞他的耳朵。每个人都看着我，好像在说，呃，你刚才说了什么？

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:15.910
<v David Kestenbaum>Wait. Why do they mess with his ears?
<v David Kestenbaum>等等。他们为什么要乱搞他的耳朵？

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:26.930
<v Jeffrey Lewis>We think they make them smaller. So I guess he feels like his ears are too big? I don't know. It's a weird thing to be worried about.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我们认为他们把耳朵改小了。所以我猜他觉得自己的耳朵太大了？我不知道。担心这个真是件奇怪的事。

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:47.390
<v David Kestenbaum>That is the kind of microanalysis Jeffrey and the people he works with do-- not just with Kim Jong Un's ears. Whenever there are new photos of a missile or there's been a nuclear test, they dig through the data in a really smart way. I've even gotten attached to the theme song, which has this upbeat time bomb thing going on. It's from a German punk band.
<v David Kestenbaum>这就是杰弗里和他共事的人所做的微观分析——不仅仅是针对金正恩的耳朵。每当有新的导弹照片或进行核试验时，他们都会以非常聪明的方式挖掘数据。我甚至对主题曲产生了感情，它有一种欢快的定时炸弹的感觉。它来自一支德国朋克乐队。

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:54.440
<v Jeffrey Lewis>You are listening to the Arms Control Wonk podcast, the leading podcast on arms control, nonproliferation, and disarmament.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>你正在收听的是《军备控制专家》播客，这是关于军备控制、防扩散和裁军的领先播客。

00:10:54.440 --> 00:11:12.950
<v David Kestenbaum>One of the things they spend a lot of time trying to sort out on the podcast is what is real. It can be hard to tell. North Korea will put out some propaganda video saying, "Another successful missile launch." But if you look closer, it is not always what it seems. Here's a clip from the podcast where Jeffrey is talking to one of his grad students.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们在播客中花了很多时间试图弄清楚什么是真实的。这很难判断。朝鲜会发布一些宣传视频，声称“又一次成功的导弹发射”。但如果你仔细看，它并不总是看起来的那样。这是播客中的一段片段，杰弗里正在和他的研究生交谈。

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:26.480
<v Dave Schmerler>So this Korean group in Japan that releases all this North Korean propaganda released a little video which they claimed to be footage of the submarine launch but turned out was not.
<v Dave Schmerler>所以这个在日本的韩国团体发布了所有这些朝鲜宣传材料，发布了一段小视频，他们声称是潜艇发射的镜头，但事实证明并非如此。

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:40.710
<v David Kestenbaum>Launching a missile from a submarine would be a big deal because, if they can put a nuclear weapon on a sub, it's harder to destroy. And they might be able to sneak it closer to US shores. So the video gets posted online and Jeffrey says, looks pretty impressive.
<v David Kestenbaum>从潜艇发射导弹将是一件大事，因为如果他们能把核武器放在潜艇上，就更难摧毁了。而且他们可能能够将其悄悄靠近美国海岸。所以视频发布在网上，杰弗里说，看起来相当令人印象深刻。

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:51.410
<v Jeffrey Lewis>They basically show the top of the tube, where the missile is, underwater. And the hatch is open, and the missile pops up, and it goes rushing through the water.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>他们基本上展示了管子的顶部，也就是导弹所在的水下位置。舱口打开，导弹弹出来，在水中冲刺。

00:11:51.410 --> 00:12:08.840
<v David Kestenbaum>But the grad student, Dave Schmerler, looks at this and thinks something doesn't seem right. So he just starts googling submarine launch footage. And one of the first things he finds on YouTube is this thing that looks very similar. And I mean very similar. Jeffrey showed me the video clip so I could compare.
<v David Kestenbaum>但研究生戴夫·施默勒看着这个，觉得有些不对劲。所以他开始在谷歌上搜索潜艇发射的镜头。他在YouTube上发现的第一件事就是看起来非常相似的东西。我是说非常相似。杰弗里给我看了视频片段，以便我比较。

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:11.570
<v David Kestenbaum>OK, so here's the North Korean one.
<v David Kestenbaum>好的，这是朝鲜的那个。

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:17.890
<v Reporter>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Reporter>[韩语演讲]

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:20.130
<v David Kestenbaum>Oh, so it's footage from underwater.
<v David Kestenbaum>哦，所以这是水下的镜头。

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:20.250
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Yeah.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>是的。

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:20.600
<v David Kestenbaum>Wow.
<v David Kestenbaum>哇。

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:22.240
<v Jeffrey Lewis>It's this crazy underwater.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>这是疯狂的水下镜头。

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:23.080
<v David Kestenbaum>That's impressive.
<v David Kestenbaum>这令人印象深刻。

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:23.950
<v Jeffrey Lewis>It looks cool, right?
<v Jeffrey Lewis>看起来很酷，对吧？

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:29.860
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, yeah. It looks cool.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，是的。看起来很酷。

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:32.170
<v David Kestenbaum>OK. So here's the other one.
<v David Kestenbaum>好的。这是另一个。

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:36.130
<v Announcer>--and a new submarine, faster, quieter--
<v Announcer>——以及一艘新的潜艇，更快、更安静——

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:39.700
<v David Kestenbaum>The other one is an old video of a US missile launch.
<v David Kestenbaum>另一个是美国导弹发射的旧视频。

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:41.030
<v David Kestenbaum>They took our footage.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们用了我们的镜头。

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:44.190
<v Announcer>--then the Poseidon, or C3 missile--
<v Announcer>——然后是波塞冬或C3导弹——

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:45.850
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Yeah. It's the same, right?
<v Jeffrey Lewis>是的。是一样的，对吧？

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:48.110
<v David Kestenbaum>I mean, it's exactly the same.
<v David Kestenbaum>我的意思是，完全一样。

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:50.470
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Oh, yeah. And they do this all the time.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>哦，是的。他们一直这样做。

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:54.370
<v David Kestenbaum>Do you think they actually launched a missile from a submarine that day?
<v David Kestenbaum>你认为他们那天真的从潜艇上发射了导弹吗？

00:12:54.370 --> 00:13:03.820
<v Jeffrey Lewis>I think they may have popped up a missile from a barge, but I don't think it flew. I don't think it worked. I don't think it involved a submarine.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我认为他们可能从驳船上弹出了导弹，但我不认为它飞了。我不认为它起作用了。我不认为它涉及潜艇。

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:07.730
<v David Kestenbaum>So basically, none of what they were saying happened happened?
<v David Kestenbaum>所以基本上，他们说的发生的事情根本没有发生？

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:09.430
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Right.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>对。

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:28.780
<v David Kestenbaum>You can see why it would be easy not to take them too seriously. The thing that Jeffrey has noticed about North Korea's claims, though, is that there is always some truth to them. Like, OK, maybe they didn't launch a missile from a sub, but they're working on it. And maybe that wasn't an actual hydrogen bomb they tested, but it was probably something else worrying.
<v David Kestenbaum>你可以看出为什么很容易不把他们太当回事。然而，杰弗里注意到朝鲜的主张中总是包含一些事实。比如，好吧，也许他们没有从潜艇上发射导弹，但他们正在努力。也许他们测试的不是真正的氢弹，但可能是一些令人担忧的其他东西。

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:42.310
<v David Kestenbaum>In listening to this podcast, you realize how relentlessly the North Koreans have been working at this stuff over the years. It's this running audio diary of North Korea's military program. Missile test after missile test. This is from 2014.
<v David Kestenbaum>在收听这个播客时，你意识到多年来朝鲜在这些事情上是多么不懈地努力。这是朝鲜军事项目的运行音频日记。导弹试验一个接一个。这是2014年的。

00:13:42.310 --> 00:14:04.830
<v Jeffrey Lewis>The pace at which they are testing is totally unprecedented. It's a little bit hard to count them all up because there have been so many tests. More of those tests this year than certainly any year I know, and maybe than ever. I mean, it is a lot of missile tests.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>他们测试的步伐是完全史无前例的。因为测试太多了，很难把它们都数清楚。今年测试的次数肯定比我所知的任何一年都要多，也许比以往任何一年都多。我的意思是，有很多导弹试验。

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:18.360
<v David Kestenbaum>I had always imagined we'd figure something out. We'd reach some agreement with North Korea, or we'd do something before things got this bad. How did we get here? This was a half hour discussion, but I'll distill it down for you.
<v David Kestenbaum>我一直以为我们会想出办法。在事情变得这么糟糕之前，我们会与朝鲜达成某种协议，或者我们会做些什么。我们是怎么走到这一步的？这是一次半小时的讨论，但我为你提炼一下。

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:36.240
<v David Kestenbaum>Jeffrey says our best chance to stop this was probably in the late '90s. This was before North Korea had nuclear bombs. Jeffrey points out it's always easier to get someone to give up something that they don't have yet. The US-- this was the Clinton administration-- and North Korea had negotiated something called the Agreed Framework.
<v David Kestenbaum>杰弗里说我们阻止这一点的最佳机会可能是在90年代末。那是在朝鲜拥有核弹之前。杰弗里指出，让人放弃他们还没有拥有的东西总是更容易的。美国——当时是克林顿政府——和朝鲜谈判达成了一个名为《框架协议》的东西。

00:14:36.240 --> 00:15:13.490
<v David Kestenbaum>North Korea would shut down its nuclear reactor that could produce plutonium for bombs. In exchange, they would get two new nuclear power plants, a different type that would make electricity and would be harder to get plutonium out of for bombs. North Korea would also get fuel oil to tide them over until the new reactors could be built. And we'd try to resume normal economic and diplomatic relations. But some Republican members of Congress felt like we were appeasing what was a brutal regime, so ending sanctions was a nonstarter. We were slow in fulfilling our part of the deal. Oil shipments were late. There were also delays building the new reactors, which were to be paid for mostly by South Korea and Japan.
<v David Kestenbaum>朝鲜将关闭其可以生产炸弹用钚的核反应堆。作为交换，他们将获得两座新的核电站，这种类型更难从中获取制造炸弹的钚。朝鲜还将获得燃料油以度过难关，直到新反应堆建成。我们将尝试恢复正常的经济和外交关系。但一些共和党国会议员觉得我们是在安抚一个残暴的政权，所以结束制裁是免谈的。我们在履行交易方面很慢。石油运输延迟了。建设新反应堆也有延误，这主要由韩国和日本出资。

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:39.270
<v David Kestenbaum>On the North Korea side, it looked like they might still be working on a bomb. In 2002, the CIA said North Korea was building centrifuges, big spinning tubes that could enrich uranium. They certainly had them by 2010, when a US weapons scientist, Sig Hecker, was invited over for a visit. The North Koreans actually had him over a bunch. They took him to this huge building as long as a football field. It was filled with centrifuges. This is from a talk he's given.
<v David Kestenbaum>在朝鲜方面，看起来他们可能仍在制造炸弹。2002年，中情局称朝鲜正在建造离心机，即可以浓缩铀的大型旋转管。到2010年，当美国武器科学家西格·赫克受邀访问时，他们肯定已经拥有了这些。朝鲜人其实邀请他去过很多次。他们带他去了这栋像足球场一样长的大楼。里面装满了离心机。这是他的一次演讲片段。

00:15:39.270 --> 00:16:09.120
<v Sig Hecker>When I went up to the second floor and I looked into the centrifuge hall through this glass, I said, wow, 2,000 centrifuges in there. They must have noticed that my jaw dropped all the way down to here. It was just totally mind-boggling. I thought it was more like a garage operation, where they had made a few of their own or whatever. Instead, this was different. Control room, all flat-paneled LEDs display, flat-panel computers. It was unbelievable.
<v Sig Hecker>当我走到二楼，透过这块玻璃看着离心机大厅时，我说，哇，里面有2000台离心机。他们一定注意到我的下巴掉到了这里。这简直令人难以置信。我以为这更像是车库操作，他们自己做了几个或者别的什么。相反，这是不同的。控制室，所有平面LED显示屏，平板电脑。这令人难以置信。

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:14.220
<v David Kestenbaum>Jeffrey Lewis says, looking back, we should have found some way to make the agreement work.
<v David Kestenbaum>杰弗里·刘易斯说，回顾过去，我们应该找到某种方法让协议奏效。

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:36.000
<v Jeffrey Lewis>We blew it. Yeah, we blew it in a pretty comprehensive, complex way. They didn't help things because they didn't trust us, and they definitely-- cheated is a word with a bunch of moral tones to it, but they didn't make it easy. So now, I think we're at a point where they have these nuclear weapons, and they're not giving them up.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我们搞砸了。是的，我们以一种相当全面、复杂的方式搞砸了。他们没有提供帮助，因为他们不信任我们，而且他们绝对——作弊是一个带有道德色彩的词，但他们并没有让事情变得简单。所以现在，我认为我们处于一个他们拥有这些核武器且不会放弃它们的阶段。

00:16:36.000 --> 00:17:02.580
<v David Kestenbaum>There have been attempts at negotiation over the years, but usually the US has insisted that North Korea give up its nukes, basically wanting to go back to those days before North Korea had a bomb. North Korea, understandably, has said no. Jeffrey says Kim Jong Un believes that, as long as he has nukes and missiles to launch them, no one will mess with him. And he's kind of right.
<v David Kestenbaum>多年来曾尝试过谈判，但通常美国坚持要求朝鲜放弃核武器，基本上是想回到朝鲜拥有炸弹之前的日子。朝鲜理所当然地说不。杰弗里说金正恩相信，只要他拥有核武器和发射导弹，就没人会来找他麻烦。他算是有道理的。

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:15.540
<v David Kestenbaum>When North Korea did its first nuclear test in 2006, it was basically a dud. Jeffrey says his reaction at the time was, they are the worst ever at this. But they've gotten better and done more tests since then.
<v David Kestenbaum>当朝鲜在2006年进行第一次核试验时，基本上是个哑弹。杰弗里说他当时的反应是，他们在这方面是有史以来最差的。但从那时起，他们已经改进并进行了更多的测试。

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:49.820
<v David Kestenbaum>North Korea has been pretty clear about what it wants to be able to do with its bombs. A few years ago, North Korea released this propaganda photo. It was taken inside some military facility. And in the background, on the wall, was this map. If you zoomed in, you could see these lines that terminate at cities in the US, presumably the ones North Korea would like to be able to strike with nuclear missiles. The photo is well-known in analyst circles. It's sometimes called the Map of Death. And then earlier this year, North Korea released an even more remarkable photo-- Kim Jong Un posing with this shiny thing.
<v David Kestenbaum>朝鲜已经很清楚它想用炸弹做什么。几年前，朝鲜发布了这张宣传照片。它是在某个军事设施内拍摄的。在背景的墙上，有这张地图。如果你放大看，你可以看到这些终点指向美国城市的线，大概就是朝鲜希望能够用核导弹打击的那些城市。这张照片在分析圈里很有名。它有时被称为死亡地图。今年早些时候，朝鲜发布了一张更引人注目的照片——金正恩拿着这个闪亮的东西拍照。

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:58.370
<v Jeffrey Lewis>I was like, holy shit, that's a nuclear weapon. Foreign leaders do not pose with nuclear weapons. That is just not a thing.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我当时心想，天哪，那是核武器。外国领导人不会拿着核武器拍照。这根本不是一回事。

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:00.130
<v David Kestenbaum>Just describe what the photo looks like.
<v David Kestenbaum>描述一下这张照片是什么样的。

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:35.450
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Yeah. He's in a missile plant. And he's in this crazy getup, which is kind of an allusion to his grandfather. So he's got like the Russian fur hat and this big coat with this fur collar. And he got these silly eyeglasses that sometimes he wears. And so he looks ridiculous. And in front of him is this spherical object. Actually, it's not spherical. It's a specific type of geometric pattern, but whatever. It's this big-- it looks like a disco ball.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>是的。他在一个导弹工厂里。他穿着这套疯狂的服装，有点暗示他的祖父。他戴着那种俄罗斯皮帽，穿着这种大衣领的大外套。他戴着这种愚蠢的眼镜，有时他会戴。所以他看起来很荒谬。在他面前是这个球形物体。其实，它不是球形的。它是一种特定的几何图案，但随便吧。它是这个大的——看起来像个迪斯科球。

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:47.150
<v David Kestenbaum>If that's an actual bomb, he says it's small enough to fit on a missile. This picture, of course, merited an entire podcast and included a rant Jeffrey delivers in lots of them about denial.
<v David Kestenbaum>如果那是一个真正的炸弹，他说它足够小，可以装进导弹里。这张照片当然值得整个播客讨论，并包括杰弗里在很多播客中关于否认的咆哮。

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:49.860
<v Jeffrey Lewis>You know what my favorite part about this whole conversation has been?
<v Jeffrey Lewis>你知道我最喜欢整个对话的哪一部分吗？

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.210
<v Aaron Stein>What?
<v Aaron Stein>什么？

00:18:52.210 --> 00:19:42.020
<v Jeffrey Lewis>I have noticed that there is this incredible reluctance to believe that the North Koreans have actually developed nuclear weapons. So every time the North does a nuclear test-- you know, I have some South Korean reporter asking me like, well, is it possible that they just filled a mountain with conventional explosives? And when you step back from it, it's like, oh, wow. They've done four nuclear tests. Three times they have paraded road mobile ICBMs down the streets of Pyongyang. They put up a map of the United States with targets on it that said Mainland Strike Plan. And now Kim Jong Un is standing in front of-- it's probably a mockup, but nonetheless-- a nuclear weapon. And people are like, what do you think they're saying? It seems pretty frickin clear to me.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我注意到人们有一种难以置信的抵触情绪，不愿相信朝鲜真的研制出了核武器。所以每次朝鲜进行核试验——你知道，我有韩国记者问我，好吧，他们是否可能只是用常规炸药填满了山？当你退后一步看时，就像是，哦，哇。他们已经进行了四次核试验。三次他们在大街上游行了公路移动洲际弹道导弹。他们贴出了一张带有目标的美国地图，上面写着“大陆打击计划”。现在金正恩站在——这可能是一个模型，但尽管如此——核武器前面。人们说，你认为他们在说什么？对我来说，这非常清楚。

00:19:42.020 --> 00:19:52.650
<v David Kestenbaum>Jeffrey says, through all this, we have kind of underestimated the North Koreans maybe because they seem so goofy. Or maybe because it's easier to do that than to admit the truth.
<v David Kestenbaum>杰弗里说，通过所有这些，我们有点低估了朝鲜人，也许是因为他们看起来太傻了。或者也许是因为承认真相比这更容易。

00:19:52.650 --> 00:20:38.520
<v Jeffrey Lewis>I actually had a big argument with-- I shouldn't call it an argument. I had a big conversation with a Japanese government official about this, where we were talking about, oh, I don't know, some missile test. I mean, there have been so many at this point. And he was sort of saying like, well, we're going to have to give sanctions some more time to work. I'm just like, you don't have more time. It's like, no, I think we'll give it a couple of years and, you know, we'll see where we are. And it's like, in a couple of years, they could have a thermonuclear weapon on an ICBM. Maybe it won't be all worked out. Maybe there will still be some problems with it, but I don't think you've got a couple of years, chief.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我其实和一位日本政府官员就此进行了激烈的争论——我不应该称之为争论。我进行了深入的对话，我们当时正在谈论，哦，我不知道，某种导弹测试。我的意思是，此时此刻测试已经太多了。他有点像在说，嗯，我们将不得不给制裁更多时间来发挥作用。我只是说，你没有更多时间了。就好像，不，我认为我们会给它几年时间，你知道，我们会看看我们在哪里。就好像，几年后，他们可能会在洲际弹道导弹上拥有热核武器。也许它不会完全解决。也许仍然会有一些问题，但我不认为你有几年时间，头儿。

00:20:38.520 --> 00:21:03.170
<v Jeffrey Lewis>I was kind of beating him up, and he was taking it pretty well. And I think the reason he was taking it pretty well is because he's a government official. He's got a job to do. And I think he kind of knows that they don't actually know what the heck they're doing. But what kind of government trots out before its citizens and says, hey, you know this really tough problem that terrifies you? We don't have the slightest idea what to do. Nobody does that.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我有点在教训他，而他接受得相当好。我认为他接受得相当好是因为他是一名政府官员。他有一份工作要做。我认为他有点知道他们实际上不知道他们到底在做什么。但什么样的政府会走到公民面前说，嘿，你知道这个让你恐惧的棘手问题吗？我们一点也不知道该怎么办。没人会那么做。

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:13.640
<v David Kestenbaum>Earlier this month, when North Korea, for the first time, tested an actual intercontinental ballistic missile that could reach Alaska, it was all over the news. Everyone was kind of freaking out.
<v David Kestenbaum>本月早些时候，当朝鲜首次测试了一枚能够到达阿拉斯加的真正洲际弹道导弹时，新闻里全是这个。每个人都有点吓坏了。

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:15.860
<v Reporter>The new provocation from North Korea.
<v Reporter>来自朝鲜的新挑衅。

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.980
<v Reporter>North Korea's recent test launch of a missile that could reach the US.
<v Reporter>朝鲜最近试射了一枚能够到达美国的导弹。

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:21.910
<v Reporter>This is North Korea's most successful missile test--
<v Reporter>这是朝鲜最成功的导弹测试——

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:24.990
<v Reporter>--which Kim Jong Un called a birthday gift to the US.
<v Reporter>——金正恩称其为给美国的生日礼物。

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:49.740
<v David Kestenbaum>But regular listeners to the podcast kind of knew it was coming because just two months ago, North Korea had tested a missile that went pretty damn far-- 1,300 miles up into space and back. So when the ICBM test actually came, I figured Jeffrey's reaction would be a shrug. Like, we told you so. But he was kind of shocked, too. Even though he knew it was coming, just the reality of it was shocking.
<v David Kestenbaum>但播客的常客们大概知道它要来了，因为就在两个月前，朝鲜测试了一枚飞得非常远的导弹——飞入太空1300英里然后返回。所以当洲际弹道导弹测试真的来临时，我以为杰弗里的反应会是耸耸肩。就像，我们告诉过你。但他也有点震惊。尽管他知道它要来了，但这本身就是令人震惊的。

00:21:49.740 --> 00:22:07.160
<v Jeffrey Lewis>This one can hit the United States with a nuclear weapon. People keep saying Alaska, but that's people making themselves feel better. It demonstrated the range to go to Alaska. That doesn't mean that that is its maximum range. We've been modeling it, and it looks a lot better than that.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>这一个可以用核武器击中美国。人们一直在说阿拉斯加，但那是人们让自己感觉好受些。它证明了去阿拉斯加的射程。这并不意味着那是它的最大射程。我们一直在建模，看起来比那个好得多。

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:08.570
<v David Kestenbaum>Meaning it can go further?
<v David Kestenbaum>意思是它可以飞得更远？

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:10.300
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Yeah, it probably can hit New York.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>是的，它可能可以击中纽约。

00:22:10.300 --> 00:22:12.060
<v David Kestenbaum>It probably can hit New York?
<v David Kestenbaum>它可能可以击中纽约？

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:18.410
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Yeah. This last one went seven times higher than the ISS.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>是的。最后这次比国际空间站高七倍。

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:20.630
<v David Kestenbaum>Seven times higher than the International Space Station?
<v David Kestenbaum>比国际空间站高七倍？

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:23.220
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Yeah, seven times higher.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>是的，高七倍。

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:25.520
<v David Kestenbaum>So this was significantly further than that other one?
<v David Kestenbaum>所以这比另一个明显远得多？

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:36.790
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah. This is a hell of a missile.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>哦，是的。哦，是的。这是一枚了不起的导弹。

00:22:36.790 --> 00:22:48.430
<v David Kestenbaum>It seems like North Korea might just keep doing this-- setting off bigger and bigger atomic bombs, launching huge missiles. Talking to Jeffrey, I realized I had this question. What exactly does North Korea want?
<v David Kestenbaum>看起来朝鲜可能只会继续这样做——引爆越来越大的原子弹，发射巨大的导弹。和杰弗里谈话时，我意识到我有这个问题。朝鲜到底想要什么？

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:51.340
<v Jeffrey Lewis>North Korea wants to be treated like a normal country.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>朝鲜想要被当作一个正常的国家来对待。

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:52.630
<v David Kestenbaum>Meaning what?
<v David Kestenbaum>意味着什么？

00:22:52.630 --> 00:23:09.560
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Meaning diplomatic recognition and no sanctions and Kim Jong Un gets invited to state dinners. They just want to be-- they want the Kim family to be treated like a legitimate leadership.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>意味着外交承认和没有制裁，金正恩被邀请参加国宴。他们只是想要——他们希望金氏家族被当作一个合法的领导层来对待。

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:38.500
<v David Kestenbaum>And that is the impossible spot we are in. This is a government the United Nations Human Rights Council says is guilty of crimes against humanity, including extermination, murder, enslavement, torture, imprisonment, rape. The list goes on. Kim Jong Un's people are starving. They're cut off from the outside world. We just ignore all that? Invite him to the White House for a state dinner? Jeffrey comes down firmly on one side of that question.
<v David Kestenbaum>这就是我们所处的不可能的境地。这是一个被联合国人权理事会称犯有危害人类罪的政府，包括灭绝、谋杀、奴役、酷刑、监禁、强奸。名单还在继续。金正恩的人民正在挨饿。他们与外界隔绝。我们就忽略这一切？邀请他去白宫参加国宴？杰弗里在那个问题上坚定地站在一边。

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:56.560
<v Jeffrey Lewis>I think the answer is you have to do it, right? That if you're not going to topple the regime-- and there would be all kinds of reasons you would not want to do that-- then you've just got to suck it up and do it. But the politics of it stink. And you can see why no president is willing to do it.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>我认为答案是你必须这样做，对吧？如果你不打算推翻这个政权——而且你不想那样做会有各种各样的原因——那么你只需要忍受并去做。但其中的政治令人作呕。你可以看出为什么没有总统愿意这样做。

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:00.030
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah. That's just like-- I mean, that's just terrible.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。那简直就像——我的意思是，那简直太可怕了。

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:27.720
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Yeah. Welcome to my world! So I settle for things like, hey, how about this? How about we knock off some of the military exercises and we just see if they'll stop testing nuclear weapons and missiles? How about we do that? It would just try to avoid a nuclear war for the next couple of years. And even then people are like, we can't stop military exercises. You're giving into a terrible dictator. Well, yeah, I guess I kind of am.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>是的。欢迎来到我的世界！所以我满足于像这样的事情，嘿，这个怎么样？我们停止一些军事演习，看看他们是否会停止测试核武器和导弹，怎么样？我们这样做怎么样？它只是试图避免未来几年的核战争。即便如此，人们还是说，我们不能停止军事演习。你是在向一个可怕的独裁者屈服。嗯，是的，我想我确实是。

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:32.410
<v David Kestenbaum>Do you think you're more comfortable with the idea of being blackmailed than other people?
<v David Kestenbaum>你认为你比其他人更能接受被勒索的想法吗？

00:24:32.410 --> 00:25:09.520
<v Jeffrey Lewis>Yeah, of course I am. Blackmail is this very specific word that has a very specific moral connotation to it. But the reality is that the North Koreans have interests, and they are very ruthless in pursuing those interests. And as unpleasant as they are-- and they're unpleasant-- we don't have the ability to make them go away.
<v Jeffrey Lewis>是的，当然。勒索是一个非常具体的词，具有非常具体的道德含义。但现实是朝鲜有利益，他们在追求这些利益时非常无情。尽管他们令人不快——他们确实令人不快——我们没有能力让他们消失。

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:38.540
<v David Kestenbaum>After nuclear weapons came along in World War II, there were two views. One was that, hey, these are just bigger bombs. But the other view was, no, these are an entirely different category of weapon. You build them precisely so that no one will use them. Which is a strange idea, but it's worked so far, and presumably it would be the same with North Korea. In a way, where you stand on this issue comes down to the question, how crazy do you think Kim Jong Un is?
<v David Kestenbaum>二战后核武器出现，有两种观点。一种是，嘿，这些只是更大的炸弹。但另一种观点是，不，这些是完全不同类别的武器。你制造它们正是为了不让任何人使用它们。这是一个奇怪的想法，但目前为止很有效，推测在朝鲜身上也会一样。在某种程度上，你在这个问题上的立场归结为这个问题，你认为金正恩有多疯狂？

00:25:38.540 --> 00:26:07.700
<v David Kestenbaum>Jeffrey says he does not stay up at night worrying about North Korea launching a nuclear missile at a US city. From everything he's seen, Kim Jong Un seems to be pretty rational. Ruthless and determined to stay in power, but rational. All the tests seem well-planned out, like the countries before that have pursued nuclear weapons. The Map of Death, him posing with the disco ball, that all seems very calculated, an attempt to show the world and the people of North Korea that he is in charge and he's not going away.
<v David Kestenbaum>杰弗里说他晚上不会因为朝鲜向美国城市发射核导弹而彻夜难眠。从他所看到的一切来看，金正恩似乎相当理性。无情且决心保持权力，但理性。所有的测试似乎都计划得很好，就像之前追求核武器的国家一样。死亡地图，他拿着迪斯科球拍照，这一切看起来都经过精心计算，试图向世界和朝鲜人民表明他是负责人的，他不会离开。

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:27.860
<v David Kestenbaum>Jeffrey says the real people in danger here are probably not us. It's the people stuck living in North Korea. That is another thing nuclear weapons can do. They change the diplomatic calculus in a way, I think, that means the bad guys sometimes win.
<v David Kestenbaum>杰弗里说这里真正处于危险中的人可能不是我们。是被困在朝鲜生活的人。这是核武器可以做的另一件事。我认为它们改变了外交算计，这意味着坏人有时会赢。

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:47.340
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum is one of the producers of our program. The podcast Arms Control Wonk does seem to have at least a few listeners in North Korea. Their episode "Another North Korean Nuclear Test" was downloaded five times from there.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆是我们节目的制片人之一。《军备控制专家》播客在朝鲜似乎确实有几个听众。他们的剧集“又一次朝鲜核试验”在那里被下载了五次。

00:26:47.340 --> 00:27:04.600
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "MARROW" BY ANI DIFRANCO]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 阿妮·迪弗兰科的《MARROW》]

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:16.390
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, Jon Ronson gets real with people who are specifically paid not to get real. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，乔恩·罗森将与那些专门被花钱请来不去表现真实的人进行真实的交流。我们节目继续后，从芝加哥公共广播电台收听。

00:27:16.390 --> 00:27:29.610
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, To Be Real. We have stories about people breaking through the polite, boring, everyday fog of social acceptability and polite denial of things that none of us really want to face and getting real.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目主题：表现真实。我们讲述的是人们突破社会认可和礼貌否认的平庸、无聊、日常迷雾，这些迷雾是没人真正想面对的，从而实现真实的各种故事。

00:27:29.610 --> 00:27:34.430
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, The Lie Becomes the Truth.
<v Ira Glass>我们已经到了节目的第二幕。第二幕，谎言变成了真理。

00:27:34.430 --> 00:27:52.720
<v Ira Glass>A couple of weeks ago, when we did an episode of our show on magicians, researching what's going on in that world, I was fascinated by a few magicians who take the premise of magic, that it's all about deception, and try to stand that on its head to get to something utterly real, unfaked, emotional, truthful.
<v Ira Glass>几周前，当我们做关于魔术师的节目时，研究那个世界正在发生什么，我被几个魔术师迷住了，他们采用了魔术的前提，即一切都是关于欺骗，并试图将其颠倒过来，以获得某种绝对真实、未经伪造、情感化的、真实的东西。

00:27:52.720 --> 00:28:45.680
<v Ira Glass>There are two magicians in particular I was interested in. The first was David Blaine, who I think you might know who this is. He's been doing TV specials for 20 years. And I was hanging around Blaine one night at dinner at a restaurant, and there was this moment where a friend of his said to him something like, David, you've told me that some of your tricks these days aren't even tricks, like when you eat glass. And Blaine was like, yeah. And he picks up the nearest wine glass at the table-- this was not his glass; somebody else had been drinking out of it-- and he poured their wine into another glass on the table. And then he raised the wine glass to his mouth and bit off a chunk of the glass. And then he chewed, like, really big chews, like, chomp, chomp, chomp, chomp, chomp. And it took a little time, actually, like, chomp, chomp, chomp, chomp, chomp. And then he took a huge drink of water and he swallowed. And then he opened his mouth to show us, you know, all gone.
<v Ira Glass>我特别感兴趣的是两位魔术师。第一位是大卫·布莱恩，我想你可能知道他是谁。他做了20年的电视特别节目。一天晚上我在餐馆吃晚饭时和布莱恩在一起，当时他的一个朋友对他说，大卫，你告诉过我，你这些天的一些把戏甚至不是把戏，比如你吃玻璃的时候。布莱恩说，是的。然后他拿起桌上最近的一个酒杯——这不是他的杯子；别人一直在喝——他把他们的酒倒进了桌上的另一个杯子里。然后他把酒杯举到嘴边，咬掉了一块玻璃。然后他嚼了起来，真的很大力地嚼，比如，咔嚓，咔嚓，咔嚓，咔嚓，咔嚓。这实际上花了一点时间，比如，咔嚓，咔嚓，咔嚓，咔嚓，咔嚓。然后他喝了一大口水，吞了下去。然后他张开嘴给我们看，你知道，全没了。

00:28:45.680 --> 00:29:04.420
<v Ira Glass>It's not a trick. He just eats glass, grinds it down to little specks, I guess, and swallows it. He said the inside of your mouth gets a little cut up, but it's not too bad. And there's a scene that they filmed for Blaine's most recent TV special where he's sitting in a dentist chair and he talks to his dentist about what this is doing to his teeth.
<v Ira Glass>这不是魔术。他只是吃玻璃，我想他把它磨成小碎片，然后吞下去。他说你嘴巴里面会有点割伤，但还算好。在为布莱恩最近的电视特别节目拍摄的一个场景中，他坐在牙医椅上，和他的牙医谈论这对他的牙齿有什么影响。

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:05.850
<v David Blaine>And they're getting bad.
<v David Blaine>而且情况正在变糟。

00:29:05.850 --> 00:29:06.200
<v Dentist>Yes.
<v Dentist>是的。

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:09.030
<v David Blaine>Because I feel hot and cold much stronger now.
<v David Blaine>因为我觉得现在对冷热的感知强多了。

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:13.520
<v Dentist>Well, that's because glass is not a normal food that you're supposed to eat.
<v Dentist>嗯，那是因为玻璃不是你应该吃的正常食物。

00:29:13.520 --> 00:29:16.190
<v Ira Glass>The dentist peers into Blaine's mouth.
<v Ira Glass>牙医盯着布莱恩的嘴看。

00:29:16.190 --> 00:29:18.260
<v Dentist>So have you been eating this on your right side of your mouth more than your left?
<v Dentist>所以你吃这个的时候是在你嘴的右侧比较多吗？

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:20.710
<v David Blaine>Yeah, only on the right side. That's so weird.
<v David Blaine>然后发生了这件有趣的事情。因为他是专业魔术师，也就是说他的工作是欺骗人，当他实际上在做这些不是魔术的惊人身体壮举时，人们往往认为它们是把戏。就像他在舞台上用嘴接住子弹——从真正的枪里射出的真正的子弹。他把它接在他含在嘴里的金属杯里，以慢动作拍摄子弹飞行的过程，拍摄他的朋友们因为他甚至试图这样做而感到不安，但仍然有些人认为，不，那不是真的。

00:29:20.710 --> 00:29:22.950
<v Dentist>You've worn your teeth down on the right much worse than the left.
<v Dentist>你的牙齿右边磨损得比左边严重得多。

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:23.570
<v David Blaine>Wow.
<v David Blaine>哇。

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:24.020
<v Dentist>Much worse.
<v Dentist>严重得多。

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:27.320
<v David Blaine>Yeah, it started shifting to the right because the left was hurting so much.
<v David Blaine>是的，因为左边疼得太厉害，所以开始转向右边。

00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:34.360
<v Dentist>Well, your right is much worse than your left. This is totally worn away. All the enamel's gone from the tooth and his nerve is almost exposed.
<v Dentist>嗯，你的右边比左边严重得多。这完全磨损了。牙齿上的所有牙釉质都没了，他的神经几乎暴露了。

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:36.060
<v Ira Glass>Blaine turns to the camera crew.
<v Ira Glass>布莱恩转向摄影组。

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:37.000
<v David Blaine>He didn't believe me--
<v David Blaine>他不相信我——

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:37.450
<v Dentist>I didn't believe him, either.
<v Dentist>我起初也不相信他。

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:37.750
<v David Blaine>--in the beginning.
<v David Blaine>——在开始时。

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:48.320
<v Dentist>I really didn't. I didn't believe him. I've never seen anyone do it, and I thought it was a h-- I thought there was some sort of trick to it, but he's eating glass.
<v Dentist>我真的不相信。我从未见过任何人这样做，我以为这有个什么——我以为这有什么把戏，但他真的是在吃玻璃。

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:55.150
<v Ira Glass>So is eating glass a thing that you know that you only can do a certain number of times in your life because you'll gradually grind--
<v Ira Glass>所以吃玻璃是你生命中只能做有限次数的事情吗，因为你会逐渐磨损——

00:29:55.150 --> 00:30:00.320
<v David Blaine>Like I was saying to the dentist, every time I drink or eat everything hot and cold, it's a nightmare.
<v David Blaine>就像我对牙医说的那样，每次我喝或吃热的和冷的东西时，那都是一场噩梦。

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:04.960
<v Ira Glass>So it's a trick like, I get to do this a certain number of times left in my life, so I've got to choose my spots.
<v Ira Glass>所以这是一个像我一生中只能做有限次数的事情，所以我必须选好时机。

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:08.670
<v David Blaine>No. No. No, no. I still do it whenever I want.
<v David Blaine>不。不。不，不。我仍然想做的时候就做。

00:30:08.670 --> 00:30:09.470
<v Ira Glass>You still do it? Really?
