00:34:54.510 --> 00:35:05.250
<v Ira Glass>But your stuff, I feel like more than any magician I've ever seen, I feel like when you do tricks, so much of it seems to be about getting a reaction out of the person, watching the person react to you.
<v Ira Glass>但你的东西，我觉得比我见过的任何魔术师都多，我觉得当你表演把戏时，其中很大一部分似乎是为了从对方身上获得反应，看着对方对你的反应。

00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:26.800
<v David Blaine>Yeah, and that's what I search for. That's the thing that I'm looking for more than anything is finding that unfiltered-- that person inside, basically, like the layers are all being peeled away. They're becoming, like, guard's down, vulnerable. You're seeing a real human being without the covers.
<v David Blaine>是的，这就是我所寻找的。这是我寻找的东西，比任何东西都重要，那就是找到那种未经过滤的——本质上的那个人，就像层层被剥离一样。他们变得，就像，防备放下，脆弱。你看到一个没有掩盖的真实人类。

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:42.020
<v David Blaine>Oh, David! What have you done? Are you a maniac? Why would you? Why would you do that?
<v David Blaine>哦，大卫！你做了什么？你是疯子吗？为什么你会？你为什么要那样做？

00:35:42.020 --> 00:36:00.690
<v Ira Glass>So Blaine loves getting that reaction from people. But interestingly, he says, when he does these physical feats, when he catches the bullet, when he holds his breath under water, when he lives in a box for weeks, he says in those moments, he feels like he's just as bare, in front of the audience, as Ricky Gervais is in that video.
<v Ira Glass>所以布莱恩喜欢从人们那里得到那种反应。但有趣的是，他说，当他做这些身体壮举时，当他接住子弹，当他在水下屏住呼吸，当他在盒子里住几周时，他说在那些时刻，他觉得自己在观众面前就像瑞奇·热维斯在视频中一样赤裸。

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:19.320
<v David Blaine>And I think part of that is what I like about the bullet catch. It's like you're dropping all of your defenses and you're exposed in front of the audience. You're like, here's this thing. I have to do what I have to do, but I'm exposed. I'm vulnerable, and I'm not bullshitting you right now. You're watching me do something that's very real. There is no lying.
<v David Blaine>而且我认为这部分是我喜欢接子弹的原因。就像你放下了所有的防备，在观众面前暴露出来。你就像，这是这回事。我必须做我必须做的事，但我暴露了。我很脆弱，我现在没有对你撒谎。你看着我做一些非常真实的事情。没有谎言。

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:34.170
<v Ira Glass>This is so the opposite of the way I think of magic. I think of magic as being so about creating a fake world where things are better than ours because they jump from here to there and they show up. You know what I mean? It never occurred to me of like, oh, the whole point for you would be--
<v Ira Glass>这和我对魔术的看法完全相反。我认为魔术是关于创造一个虚假的世界，事物比我们的更好，因为它们从这里跳到那里，然后它们出现。你知道我的意思吗？从来没想过，哦，对你来说整个重点是——

00:36:34.170 --> 00:36:34.880
<v David Blaine>The opposite.
<v David Blaine>相反。

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:42.960
<v Ira Glass>--that you get to see this real, raw emotion in these people, and then, when you're doing the stunts, that they get to see that in you.
<v Ira Glass>——你得以在这些人身上看到这种真实的、原始的情感，然后，当你做特技时，他们得以在你身上看到这一点。

00:36:42.960 --> 00:37:16.650
<v David Blaine>Yeah. Yeah, it's like you're not protected. You're not covered. You're not hidden. And as a magician, you're always covered up. You're always hiding everything, right? That's what magic is. You're powerful and almighty, but to me, the interesting part of a magician is not that part. It's the exposure. It's like him being vulnerable and open in front of the audience because that's what we really care about.
<v David Blaine>是的。是的，就像你没有受到保护。你没有被覆盖。你没有被隐藏。作为一个魔术师，你总是被覆盖。你总是隐藏一切，对吧？魔术就是这样。你强大而全能，但对我来说，魔术师有趣的部分不是那部分。是暴露。就像他在观众面前变得脆弱和开放，因为这就是我们真正关心的。

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:33.400
<v Ira Glass>Researching that magic episode, I went to see a lot of magic. David Copperfield, Penn & Teller, Criss Angel, Derren Brown, Mac King, Dan White. And my favorite thing I saw was done by a magician who, like Blaine, is trying something different with magic than most magicians do and going for something that is more real.
<v Ira Glass>研究那个魔术剧集时，我看了很多魔术。大卫·科波菲尔、潘恩和泰勒、克里斯·安吉尔、德伦·布朗、麦克·金、丹·怀特。我看到最喜欢的东西是一位魔术师做的，他和布莱恩一样，正在尝试与大多数魔术师不同的东西，追求更真实的东西。

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:53.940
<v Ira Glass>The magician's name is Derek DelGaudio. He's a close-up magician who gets awarded those prizes where magicians vote on who's the best in the world. He's invented a bunch of diabolical ways to shuffle and deal cards that give him incredible control of where cards are in the deck and let him do some mind-blowing stuff. But his new show isn't about that at all. I talked to him about it.
<v Ira Glass>魔术师的名字叫德里克·德尔高迪奥。他是一位近景魔术师，获得了那些魔术师投票选出谁是世界最佳的奖项。他发明了一堆邪恶的洗牌和发牌方式，让他对牌在牌堆里的位置有令人难以置信的控制力，让他做一些令人震惊的事情。但他的新节目根本不是关于那个的。我和他谈过。

00:37:53.940 --> 00:38:09.540
<v Ira Glass>Derek's a baby-faced guy in his early 30s with a careful, precise manner on stage and off. A guy who is especially interesting to talk to when he starts complaining about stuff which he does entertainingly and well. For instance, this is him talking about how people view magic.
<v Ira Glass>德里克是一个30出头、长着一张娃娃脸的家伙，在舞台上和台下都非常谨慎和精确。当他开始抱怨事情时，他是一个特别有趣的人，而且他抱怨得很生动。例如，这是他在谈论人们如何看待魔术。

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:31.470
<v Derek Delgaudio>There's got to be a reason that people use the word "magician" as like the highest compliment. Oh, you should taste this guy's food. He's a magician in the kitchen. Or, oh, you should see him on the basketball court. The guy's a magician out there. Oh, he's a magician. It's like, oh, Jesus, really? He's a mag-- like, an actual magician? Like, oh, god, you know?
<v Derek Delgaudio>一定有一个原因，人们用“魔术师”这个词作为最高的赞美。哦，你应该尝尝这个家伙的食物。他是厨房里的魔术师。或者，哦，你应该看看他在篮球场上的表现。那个家伙是那里的魔术师。哦，他是个魔术师。就像，哦，上帝，真的吗？他是个魔术师——就像，哦，天哪，你知道吗？

00:38:31.470 --> 00:38:38.400
<v Ira Glass>Wait. And why is that? Why is that it's a positive for everything except for actual magicians?
<v Ira Glass>等等。为什么是这样？为什么这对除了真正魔术师以外的一切都是正面的？

00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:50.010
<v Derek Delgaudio>Well, it's because a regular, traditional magic show isn't about anything other than the performer's ability to show you these things.
<v Derek Delgaudio>嗯，这是因为一个常规的、传统的魔术表演除了表演者向你展示这些东西的能力之外，什么也没有。

00:38:50.010 --> 00:39:13.640
<v Ira Glass>What he means by that is that most magic shows aren't about anything. They're just a series of amazing tricks. You do a trick. They applaud. You do another trick. They applaud. You do another trick. They applaud. And this is the thing he's trying to do differently in his new show, which is up in New York right now, called In & Of Itself. He's tried to create a show that is more than just tricks, more than people wondering how'd he do it. He's tried to create a show that's about something.
<v Ira Glass>他的意思是大多数魔术表演什么都不是。它们只是一系列惊人的把戏。你做一个把戏。他们鼓掌。你做另一个把戏。他们鼓掌。你做第三个把戏。他们鼓掌。这就是他在他的新节目中试图做得不同的事情，该节目目前正在纽约上演，名叫《内在与自身》。他试图创造一个不仅仅是把戏的节目，不仅仅是人们想知道他是怎么做的。他试图创造一个关于某种东西的节目。

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:42.420
<v Ira Glass>To achieve that, Derek doesn't even get to the first trick till eight or nine minutes in. And over the course of the show, he tells a series of stories that are sincere and personal in this way that you almost never see in any magic show. There's a story from his childhood about him and his mom. And another about something a guy said to him in a bar years ago. And another about the brief period of his life when he worked as a hustler dealing crooked poker games. All these stories are about people not seeing him as he really is.
<v Ira Glass>为了实现这一点，德里克甚至在八九分钟后才进行第一个把戏。在节目过程中，他讲述了一系列真诚和个人的故事，这种方式你在任何魔术表演中几乎都看不到。有一个关于他童年时期他和妈妈的故事。另一个关于几年前在酒吧里有人对他说的话。另一个关于他生命中短暂的一段他作为骗子经营歪门邪道扑克游戏的时间。所有这些故事都是关于人们没有看到他真实的样子。

